<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Friedrich Engels</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1820">1820</a>–<a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Engels, Friedrich Oswald</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Friedrich Engels was a German philosopher, social and political theorist and revolutionary socialist, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barmen">Barmen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, to a prosperous mercantile family. Sent to Manchester in 1842 to work in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> mill, he wrote The Condition of the Working Class in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> (1845). From 1844 he worked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a>, with whom he co-authored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto">The Communist Manifesto</a> (1848) and developed what became known as Marxism. He fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848</a>, then supported the Marx family from the family firm of Ermen &amp; Engels. After Marx died in 1883 he completed the second and third volumes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/das-kapital">Das Kapital</a>; he died of cancer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1895.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels.jpg" alt="Friedrich Engels" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Hall (1826–ca. 1898) (cropped and sepia tone removed) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_Engels_portrait_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ernest Jones, born into the landed gentry and a barrister, gave the late movement a socialist direction; he and Harney knew Karl Marx and <b>Friedrich Engels</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/chartism">Chartism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Karl Marx and <b>Friedrich Engels</b>, whose Communist Manifesto appeared in 1848, argued that the working class must take power and end the private ownership of the means of production.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Karl Marx criticised anarchism as a movement of the “petty bourgeois”, of ruined craftsmen and artisans who refused factory discipline, party leadership and state control, and <b>Friedrich Engels</b> judged its anti-authoritarianism counter-revolutionary, since in his view a revolution is by itself authoritarian.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>Karl Marx analysed the capitalist mode of production in Das Kapital (1867), and <b>Friedrich Engels</b> popularised the term.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>As the Industrial Revolution advanced, socialist critics blamed capitalism for the misery of the proletariat of factory workers, and in 1848 Marx and <b>Engels</b> offered a new definition of communism in The Communist Manifesto.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Karl Marx and <b>Friedrich Engels</b> declared in 1848 that “the working men have no country”, though Vladimir Lenin supported self-determination.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
